    <div class="section-label">Section 01</div>
    <h2>What is Log Analysis?</h2>
    <p>Imagine your computer as a busy government office. Every person who enters, every document touched, every door opened — it all gets recorded in a logbook at the front desk. <strong>Log analysis</strong> is the discipline of reading, understanding, and interrogating those logbooks to answer one fundamental question: <em>What happened, and should we be worried?</em></p>
    <p>In digital systems, logs are automatically generated records of events — actions taken by software, hardware, users, and network devices. A single web server can generate tens of thousands of log entries per hour. A corporate network can produce billions per day. The analyst's job is to turn that torrent of raw text into actionable intelligence.</p>
    <div class="pullquote">
      "Logs are the single most important source of evidence in any digital investigation. Without them, you are reconstructing a crime from memory. With them, you have a timestamped witness that never lies."
    </div>
    <p>Log analysis sits at the intersection of <strong>forensic investigation</strong>, <strong>threat detection</strong>, and <strong>compliance auditing</strong>. It is the foundation of the modern Security Operations Centre (SOC) and a core skill for any cybersecurity professional.</p>
    <h3>Why Logs Matter</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Visibility:</strong> Logs reveal what is actually happening inside systems — not what you think is happening.</li>
      <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Every action can be traced to a user, process, or IP address.</li>
      <li><strong>Incident Response:</strong> Logs let you reconstruct the attacker's kill chain after a breach.</li>
      <li><strong>Compliance:</strong> Regulations like PCI-DSS, HIPAA, and ISO 27001 mandate log retention and review.</li>
      <li><strong>Proactive Defence:</strong> Patterns in logs reveal attacks in progress — before damage is done.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- Section 2 -->
  <div class="section" id="s2">
    <div class="section-label">Section 02</div>
    <h2>A Brief History of Logging</h2>
    <p>Logging is as old as computing itself. In the 1960s and 70s, mainframe systems kept <em>job accounting logs</em> to track CPU usage and billing. These were not security tools — they were invoices. But the idea of a machine recording its own activity was planted.</p>
    <p>In the 1980s, Unix systems introduced <strong>syslog</strong>, a standardised protocol for recording system events. This became the backbone of logging on Linux and Unix-like systems worldwide, and its influence endures to this day. The syslog format — a timestamp, a hostname, a process name, and a message — seems simple, but it established a template that hundreds of logging systems still follow.</p>
    <p>The 1990s brought the internet age and with it, <strong>web server logs</strong> recording every HTTP request. Network administrators began correlating firewall logs, router logs, and server logs to track intrusions. The discipline of log analysis as a security practice was born.</p>
    <p>By the early 2000s, the sheer volume of logs had overwhelmed manual analysis. This necessity gave birth to the first <strong>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)</strong> systems — platforms designed to collect, aggregate, and automatically correlate log data at scale. Vendors like ArcSight, IBM QRadar, and Splunk emerged as market leaders.</p>
    <p>Today, the modern SOC analyst works with cloud-native SIEMs, machine-learning-based anomaly detection, and threat intelligence feeds that enrich raw log data with real-world context. But the fundamentals — understanding what a log entry means — remain unchanged and essential.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- Section 3 -->
  <div class="section" id="s3">
    <div class="section-label">Section 03</div>
    <h2>Types of Logs — The Evidence Landscape</h2>
    <p>Not all logs are created equal. Each type captures a different slice of activity on a different layer of your infrastructure. A skilled analyst knows where to look for what kind of evidence.</p>

    <h3>System Logs</h3>
    <p>System logs record the activity of the operating system itself: services starting and stopping, driver failures, kernel events, hardware errors, and scheduled task execution. On Windows, these live in the <strong>Windows Event Log</strong> under the "System" channel. On Linux, they are found in <code>/var/log/syslog</code>, <code>/var/log/messages</code>, or the systemd journal.</p>
    <div class="infobox">
      <div class="infobox-title">Analogy</div>
      <p style="margin:0;font-size:0.95rem;">Think of system logs as the building's maintenance record — they tell you when the lights flickered, when the boiler was serviced, and when the alarm system rebooted unexpectedly.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>What to look for:</strong> Unexpected service restarts, driver loads from unusual paths, system crashes (BSODs), and time synchronisation failures (an attacker may tamper with clocks to obscure their timeline).</p>

    <h3>Application Logs</h3>
    <p>Application logs are generated by individual software programs. Every web application, database, email server, and line-of-business application typically writes its own logs, often in a vendor-specific format. These logs capture errors, user actions, transactions, and debug information.</p>
    <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Apache/Nginx access and error logs, Microsoft SQL Server logs, Active Directory application logs, SAP transaction logs.</p>
    <p><strong>What to look for:</strong> Failed login attempts, SQL errors that might indicate injection, unusual data export volumes, and application-specific privilege changes.</p>

    <h3>Security Logs</h3>
    <p>Security logs are the most directly relevant to investigations. On Windows, the <strong>Security Event Log</strong> records authentication events, policy changes, object access, and privilege use. This is the primary log for identity and access investigations.</p>
    <p><strong>What to look for:</strong> Logon/logoff events, account creation and deletion, password changes, audit policy modifications, and use of sensitive privileges.</p>

    <h3>Firewall Logs</h3>
    <p>Firewalls sit at the boundary between networks and record every connection attempt — allowed and denied. A firewall log entry typically includes source IP, destination IP, destination port, protocol, and action (permit/deny). These logs are invaluable for identifying scanning, exfiltration, and command-and-control communications.</p>
    <p><strong>What to look for:</strong> Large numbers of connection denials from a single source (scanning), outbound connections to unusual IP ranges or ports, sudden spikes in egress traffic (exfiltration), and connections to known malicious IPs.</p>

    <h3>Web Server Logs</h3>
    <p>Web server logs record every HTTP/HTTPS request to a web application. The Combined Log Format used by Apache and Nginx includes timestamp, client IP, HTTP method, requested URL, response code, response size, referrer, and user agent. These logs are essential for detecting web-based attacks.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
<span class="comment"># Combined Log Format — Apache/Nginx example</span>
192.168.1.45 - admin [08/May/2026:14:22:31 +0000] <span class="string">"POST /login HTTP/1.1"</span> 401 512 <span class="string">"-"</span> <span class="string">"Mozilla/5.0"</span>
192.168.1.45 - admin [08/May/2026:14:22:32 +0000] <span class="string">"POST /login HTTP/1.1"</span> 401 512 <span class="string">"-"</span> <span class="string">"Mozilla/5.0"</span>
192.168.1.45 - admin [08/May/2026:14:22:33 +0000] <span class="string">"POST /login HTTP/1.1"</span> 200 2048 <span class="string">"-"</span> <span class="string">"Mozilla/5.0"</span>
<span class="comment"># Two 401s followed by a 200 — classic brute force then success</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What to look for:</strong> Rapid sequences of 401 (Unauthorized) responses, HTTP 500 errors in bulk (fuzzing/scanning), requests containing <code>../</code> (path traversal), SQL keywords in query strings, and unusually large POST request bodies.</p>

