  <div class="section" id="s4">
    <div class="section-label">Section 04</div>
    <h2>Windows Event IDs Critical for Investigations</h2>
    <p>Windows Security Event Log is one of the richest sources of forensic evidence available to an analyst. Every event is tagged with a numeric <strong>Event ID</strong> that identifies its type. Knowing the critical Event IDs is like knowing the Dewey Decimal System in a forensic library — it lets you find exactly what you need, fast.</p>

    <table class="event-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Event ID</th>
          <th>Name</th>
          <th>What It Records</th>
          <th>Investigative Significance</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="eid">4624</td>
          <td>Successful Logon</td>
          <td>A user account successfully authenticated to a Windows system.</td>
          <td>Baseline of normal access. Logon Type field is critical: Type 3 = Network, Type 10 = Remote Interactive (RDP). Unusual logon types from unexpected sources flag lateral movement.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="eid">4625</td>
          <td>Failed Logon</td>
          <td>A logon attempt was rejected — wrong password, account locked, or invalid account.</td>
          <td>Rapid sequences of 4625s from one source IP targeting multiple accounts = brute force. Failure reason codes reveal whether the account exists (0xC000006A = wrong password vs 0xC0000064 = unknown username).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="eid">4688</td>
          <td>Process Creation</td>
          <td>A new process was created, including the parent process and full command line (if command-line auditing is enabled).</td>
          <td>Goldmine for detecting malware execution, PowerShell abuse, and living-off-the-land (LOLBin) attacks. Look for cmd.exe spawned by Office apps, encoded PowerShell strings, and unusual parent-child process relationships.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="eid">4720</td>
          <td>User Account Created</td>
          <td>A new user account was created in the domain or locally.</td>
          <td>Attackers create backdoor accounts for persistence. Any unexpected 4720 — especially outside business hours or by a non-admin account — warrants immediate investigation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="eid">7045</td>
          <td>New Service Installed</td>
          <td>A new Windows service was installed on the system.</td>
          <td>A classic attacker persistence technique. Malware often registers itself as a service to survive reboots. Look for services with random names, paths in <code>%TEMP%</code> or unusual locations, and services installed outside change management windows.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Additional High-Value Event IDs</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>4648</strong> — Logon using explicit credentials (RunAs). Common in lateral movement when attackers use <code>runas</code> or pass-the-hash techniques.</li>
      <li><strong>4672</strong> — Special privileges assigned to logon. Indicates a user logged on with admin-equivalent rights. Follow this to track privileged sessions.</li>
      <li><strong>4698</strong> — Scheduled task created. Attackers use scheduled tasks for persistence and lateral movement. Correlate with 4688 to see what the task executes.</li>
      <li><strong>4776</strong> — Credential validation (NTLM). Captures NTLM authentication attempts, including pass-the-hash. High volumes = hash spraying.</li>
      <li><strong>1102</strong> — Audit log cleared. An attacker clearing evidence. This event itself is proof of suspicious activity. Correlate with preceding logon to identify who cleared it.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block">
<span class="comment"># Hunting for brute-force with PowerShell</span>
<span class="keyword">Get-WinEvent</span> -FilterHashtable @{
  LogName   = <span class="string">'Security'</span>
  Id        = 4625
  StartTime = (Get-Date).AddHours(-1)
} | Group-Object { $_.Properties[19].Value } |
  Where-Object Count -gt 10 |
  Sort-Object Count -Descending
<span class="comment"># Groups failed logins by source IP in the last hour
# Any IP with &gt;10 failures = potential brute force</span>
    </div>
  </div>
