  <!-- Section 5 -->
  <div class="section" id="s5">
    <div class="section-label">Section 05</div>
    <h2>What is a SIEM?</h2>
    <p>A <strong>Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)</strong> system is the nerve centre of a modern Security Operations Centre. It does what no human analyst can do manually: collect logs from hundreds of sources simultaneously, normalise them into a common format, correlate events across systems in real time, and generate alerts when suspicious patterns emerge.</p>
    <p>The term SIEM is a merger of two earlier disciplines:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>SIM (Security Information Management):</strong> Long-term storage and analysis of log data for compliance and forensics.</li>
      <li><strong>SEM (Security Event Management):</strong> Real-time monitoring, correlation, and alerting on live event streams.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Together, SIEM provides both the <em>investigative rearview mirror</em> (what happened in the past) and the <em>live dashboard</em> (what is happening right now).</p>

    <div class="infobox">
      <div class="infobox-title">Popular SIEM Platforms</div>
      <p style="margin-bottom:8px;font-size:0.9rem;">Each platform has different strengths:</p>
      <div>
        <span class="tag">Splunk</span>
        <span class="tag">IBM QRadar</span>
        <span class="tag">Microsoft Sentinel</span>
        <span class="tag">Elastic SIEM</span>
        <span class="tag">LogRhythm</span>
        <span class="tag">Securonix</span>
