  <!-- Section 6 -->
  <div class="section" id="s6">
    <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
    <h2>SIEM Core Concepts: Normalisation, Correlation &amp; Alerts</h2>

    <h3>Normalisation</h3>
    <p>Different systems log the same concept in wildly different ways. A Cisco firewall writes <code>%ASA-6-302013: Built inbound TCP connection</code>. A Windows system writes Event ID 5156. A Linux firewall writes a syslog iptables entry. They all mean "a TCP connection was established" — but they look nothing alike.</p>
    <p><strong>Normalisation</strong> is the process of mapping these diverse formats into a common schema — a universal language for events. Common normalised fields include:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>src_ip / dst_ip:</strong> Source and destination IP addresses</li>
      <li><strong>src_port / dst_port:</strong> Source and destination ports</li>
      <li><strong>user:</strong> Username associated with the event</li>
      <li><strong>timestamp:</strong> Event time in a standard format (UTC ISO 8601)</li>
      <li><strong>event_type:</strong> Categorised action (authentication, network, process, etc.)</li>
      <li><strong>outcome:</strong> Success or failure</li>
      <li><strong>host:</strong> The system where the event occurred</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Without normalisation, writing correlation rules would be impossible — you would need a different rule for every log format. With normalisation, one rule can detect brute force across Windows, Linux, web applications, and VPN gateways simultaneously.</p>

    <h3>Correlation Rules</h3>
    <p>Correlation rules are the intelligence layer of a SIEM. They define conditions that, when met across one or more log sources within a time window, trigger an alert. Think of them as <em>if-then logic applied to a river of events</em>.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
<span class="comment"># Example Correlation Rule — Brute Force Detection</span>
<span class="keyword">RULE</span> "Brute Force Login Attempt" {
  <span class="keyword">WHEN</span>:
    event.type  = <span class="string">"authentication"</span>
    event.outcome = <span class="string">"failure"</span>
  <span class="keyword">GROUP BY</span>: src_ip, target_user
  <span class="keyword">THRESHOLD</span>: count &gt; 10
  <span class="keyword">WITHIN</span>: 5 minutes
  <span class="keyword">THEN</span>: create_alert(severity="HIGH",
         message="Possible brute force from " + src_ip)
}
    </div>

    <p>Correlation rules range from simple threshold-based rules (ten failed logins in five minutes) to complex multi-stage rules that track an attacker across their entire kill chain — from initial phishing email through to data exfiltration.</p>
    <p><strong>Rule tuning</strong> is a critical skill. A poorly tuned rule generates thousands of false positives, overwhelming analysts and causing alert fatigue. A well-tuned rule fires rarely but accurately, meaning every alert deserves immediate attention.</p>

    <h3>Alerts and Triage</h3>
    <p>When a correlation rule fires, it creates an <strong>alert</strong> — a notification that something suspicious may have occurred. Alerts are classified by severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low, Informational) and should include enough context for an analyst to quickly assess whether the alert represents a genuine threat.</p>
    <p>The <strong>triage</strong> process involves asking three questions:</p>
    <ol class="styled">
      <li><strong>Is this a true positive or a false positive?</strong> Does the evidence actually support the alert?</li>
      <li><strong>What is the scope?</strong> Is this isolated to one system, or are multiple systems affected?</li>
      <li><strong>What is the urgency?</strong> Is the attack ongoing, or is this a historical indicator?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Effective triage requires deep familiarity with the environment — knowing what is normal for your organisation is essential for identifying what is abnormal.</p>
  </div>
