  <!-- Section 7 -->
  <div class="section" id="s7">
    <div class="section-label">Section 07</div>
    <h2>Log Collection Architecture</h2>
    <p>Logs are only useful if they are collected reliably and stored securely. The architecture of log collection involves several components working together.</p>

    <h3>Agents vs Agentless Collection</h3>
    <p><strong>Agent-based collection</strong> installs a lightweight software agent on each system. The agent monitors local logs and forwards events to the SIEM in near-real-time. This is more reliable and allows for richer data but requires deployment and maintenance on every endpoint. Examples: Splunk Universal Forwarder, Elastic Beats, NXLog.</p>
    <p><strong>Agentless collection</strong> uses network protocols to pull logs from systems without local software. Common methods include Windows Event Forwarding (WEF), Syslog over UDP/TCP, SNMP traps, and API integrations. Simpler to deploy but can lose events if the network is overloaded.</p>

    <h3>Log Storage and Retention</h3>
    <p>Logs must be stored long enough to support forensic investigations. Industry best practices recommend:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Hot storage (searchable):</strong> 90 days minimum — covers most incident response timelines</li>
      <li><strong>Warm storage (indexed, slower search):</strong> 90 days to 1 year</li>
      <li><strong>Cold storage (archived):</strong> 1–7 years depending on regulatory requirements</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Critically, logs stored for forensic purposes must be <strong>tamper-evident</strong>. Attackers who gain access to log systems will attempt to delete or modify entries. Cryptographic hashing, write-once storage, and centralised collection to a system the attacker cannot reach are all defence-in-depth measures.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="divider"></div>

