    <h3>Variants of Brute Force</h3>
    <div class="pattern-grid">
      <div class="pattern-card">
        <h4>Classic Brute Force</h4>
        <p>One account, many passwords at high speed. Easily detected by threshold rules. Modern systems lock accounts after 5–10 failures.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pattern-card">
        <h4>Password Spray</h4>
        <p>One common password (e.g., "Summer2026!") tried across many accounts. Bypasses per-account lockout policies. Identified by wide fan-out of failures.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pattern-card">
        <h4>Credential Stuffing</h4>
        <p>Using username/password pairs from previous data breaches. Higher success rate, lower noise. Look for geographically impossible logins.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pattern-card">
        <h4>Slow Brute Force</h4>
        <p>One attempt per hour to avoid rate limiting. Requires long-window correlation rules (24–48 hours) to detect.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
