  <!-- Section 9 -->
  <div class="section" id="s9">
    <div class="section-label">Section 09</div>
    <h2>Attack Pattern: Lateral Movement</h2>
    <p>Once an attacker has gained a foothold on one system, their next objective is to <strong>move laterally</strong> — to spread across the network, gain access to additional systems, and work their way toward high-value targets such as domain controllers, databases, or file servers storing sensitive data.</p>
    <p>Think of the network as a building. Initial compromise is getting through the front door. Lateral movement is the attacker quietly exploring each office, picking locks, and collecting keys — moving deeper into the building without triggering the alarm.</p>

    <h3>Common Lateral Movement Techniques</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Pass-the-Hash (PtH):</strong> Attackers capture the NTLM password hash from memory (using tools like Mimikatz) and use it directly for authentication without knowing the cleartext password. Generates Event IDs 4624 (Type 3) and 4776.</li>
      <li><strong>Pass-the-Ticket (PtT):</strong> Stealing Kerberos tickets from memory and using them to authenticate to other services. Related to Golden Ticket and Silver Ticket attacks.</li>
      <li><strong>Remote Services:</strong> Using legitimate remote access tools — RDP (Event 4624 Type 10), WMI (Event 4648), SMB, PowerShell Remoting — to execute commands on remote systems.</li>
      <li><strong>Scheduled Tasks / Services:</strong> Creating tasks on remote systems (Event 4698) to execute code. Persistence and lateral movement in one step.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Log Indicators of Lateral Movement</h3>
    <div class="code-block">
<span class="comment"># Indicators to hunt for across Windows Security Logs</span>

<span class="keyword">Logon Type 3</span> (Network) from unusual source to internal targets
  → Event 4624, LogonType=3, non-DC to non-DC

<span class="keyword">Explicit Credential Use</span>
  → Event 4648: "A logon was attempted using explicit credentials"

<span class="keyword">Service Installation on remote hosts</span>
  → Event 7045 on multiple systems in short window

<span class="keyword">Process Creation: PsExec/WMI artefacts</span>
  → Event 4688: C:\Windows\PSEXESVC.exe, wmiprvse.exe spawning cmd.exe

<span class="keyword">Kerberos Anomalies</span>
  → Event 4769 (Ticket Request) for accounts/services out of normal pattern
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- Section 10 -->
  <div class="section" id="s10">
    <div class="section-label">Section 10</div>
    <h2>Attack Pattern: Privilege Escalation</h2>
    <p>An attacker who compromises a standard user account has limited damage potential. <strong>Privilege escalation</strong> is the process of gaining higher-level permissions — typically local administrator or domain administrator — to unlock access to restricted systems, disable security controls, and achieve objectives at scale.</p>
    <p>There are two main categories:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Vertical escalation:</strong> Gaining higher privileges than currently held (user → admin → SYSTEM → Domain Admin).</li>
      <li><strong>Horizontal escalation:</strong> Gaining access to another account at the same privilege level (user A → user B, to access different data).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Key Log Indicators</h3>
