  <!-- Section 11 -->
  <div class="section" id="s11">
    <div class="section-label">Section 11</div>
    <h2>Building a Timeline from Multi-Source Log Data</h2>
    <p>A forensic timeline is the single most powerful analytical tool available to an incident responder. It places every relevant event in chronological order, across all available log sources, to reconstruct exactly what happened and in what sequence. Think of it as building a movie from still photographs found in different rooms of a house.</p>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Timeline Construction</h3>
    <ol class="styled">
      <li><strong>Identify the scope:</strong> What systems are potentially involved? What is the suspected timeframe?</li>
      <li><strong>Collect relevant logs:</strong> Gather Security, System, Application, firewall, web server, and proxy logs for the affected systems and time window.</li>
      <li><strong>Normalise timestamps:</strong> Ensure all logs are in the same timezone (convert everything to UTC). A single hour's offset can completely mislead an investigation.</li>
      <li><strong>Import to a common tool:</strong> Use a SIEM, spreadsheet, or dedicated timeline tool (Plaso/Log2Timeline, Timesketch) to merge all entries into one chronological stream.</li>
      <li><strong>Filter and focus:</strong> Suppress known-good activity (scheduled tasks, antivirus scans) to reduce noise. Focus on the accounts, IPs, and systems in scope.</li>
      <li><strong>Annotate and map to kill chain:</strong> Label events with their role: Initial Access, Execution, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Defence Evasion, Credential Access, Discovery, Lateral Movement, Collection, Exfiltration, Impact.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example: Reconstructed Attack Timeline</h3>
