  <!-- Section 12 -->
  <div class="section" id="s12">
    <div class="section-label">Section 12</div>
    <h2>Practical Case Study — Ransomware Intrusion</h2>
    <p>Let's walk through a realistic scenario from beginning to end, showing which logs reveal which attacker actions.</p>
    <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> An employee reports their files have been encrypted. A ransom note has appeared on the desktop. Your job is to determine how the attacker got in, what they did, and what they accessed before encrypting.</p>

    <h3>Phase 1 — Initial Access (T+0 to T+2 hours)</h3>
    <p><strong>Source: Email gateway log + Endpoint log</strong></p>
    <p>Email logs show a message received with a malicious macro-enabled Office document. The endpoint's security log (Event 4688) shows Word.exe spawning cmd.exe, which runs a PowerShell download cradle connecting to an external IP. This is the initial foothold.</p>

    <h3>Phase 2 — Persistence and Discovery (T+2 to T+6 hours)</h3>
    <p><strong>Source: Security Event Log (System channel)</strong></p>
    <p>Event 7045 shows a new service "WindowsUpdateHelper" installed from <code>C:\Users\Public\Downloads\update.exe</code>. Event 4698 shows a scheduled task created to run the same binary hourly. The attacker is establishing persistence while conducting quiet internal reconnaissance.</p>

    <h3>Phase 3 — Credential Harvesting and Lateral Movement (T+6 to T+20 hours)</h3>
    <p><strong>Source: Security Event Log (multiple hosts)</strong></p>
    <p>Events 4648 and 4624 (Type 3) across dozens of internal hosts show the attacker using harvested credentials to spread laterally via SMB. Event 4776 records show NTLM authentication from the original patient-zero machine to all hosts on the /24 subnet — a clear sign of automated credential use.</p>

    <h3>Phase 4 — Ransomware Deployment (T+22 hours)</h3>
    <p><strong>Source: Endpoint logs + Firewall logs</strong></p>
    <p>Event 4688 shows mass execution of the ransomware binary across 47 systems. Firewall logs show outbound HTTPS to a known command-and-control domain immediately preceding encryption — the attacker completing the extortion data backup before locking files.</p>

    <div class="infobox">
      <div class="infobox-title">Key Lessons from This Case Study</div>
      <ul class="styled" style="margin:0;">
        <li>Email logs identified initial access — often overlooked in SIEM collection scope</li>
        <li>Event 7045 (new service) is a critical early warning indicator</li>
        <li>The 22-hour gap between access and deployment is the detection window — SIEM correlation must be aggressive in this phase</li>
        <li>Multi-source correlation was essential — no single log source told the full story</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- Section 13 -->
  <div class="section" id="s13">
    <div class="section-label">Section 13</div>
    <h2>Common Pitfalls &amp; Analyst Tips</h2>
    <p>Experience in log analysis is largely an accumulation of lessons learned from mistakes. Here are the most common pitfalls and how to avoid them.</p>

    <h3>Pitfall 1: Trusting Timestamps Blindly</h3>
    <p>System clocks drift. Virtual machines can have wildly inaccurate clocks. Attackers deliberately manipulate system time. Always verify that all timestamps are synchronised to a trusted NTP source and convert everything to UTC before building a timeline. A 1-hour difference in timezone handling has derailed many investigations.</p>

    <h3>Pitfall 2: Alert Fatigue</h3>
    <p>An analyst who receives 1,000 alerts per day will eventually stop treating them seriously. Rule tuning is not optional — it is an ongoing operational requirement. Start with high-confidence, low-noise rules and expand incrementally. Track false positive rates and tune aggressively.</p>

    <h3>Pitfall 3: Treating Absence of Logs as Evidence of Absence</h3>
    <p>If a system isn't generating logs, you are blind on that system — you cannot conclude "nothing happened." Audit your log collection coverage regularly and treat gaps as security risks, not clean bills of health.</p>

    <h3>Pitfall 4: Analysing One Log Source in Isolation</h3>
    <p>Sophisticated attackers are aware of individual log sources and may attempt to suppress them. Always correlate across multiple sources. If a user account appears in firewall logs but has no corresponding Security Event Log entries on the destination host, that gap itself is suspicious.</p>

    <h3>Pro Tips</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Build a baseline first:</strong> Know what normal looks like before hunting for abnormal. Document typical logon hours, common service accounts, and expected network flows.</li>
      <li><strong>Use threat intelligence:</strong> Enrich IP addresses and domains in logs against threat intelligence feeds. A connection to a known C2 domain transforms a mundane log entry into a high-priority alert.</li>
      <li><strong>Document everything:</strong> Write your investigation notes contemporaneously. You will need them for incident reports, legal proceedings, and post-incident review.</li>
      <li><strong>Think like an attacker:</strong> Ask "if I were trying to do X, what would I have to do, and what logs would that generate?" then go look for exactly those logs.</li>
      <li><strong>Practice in lab environments:</strong> Set up a Windows lab, generate the attack traffic yourself, and watch the logs. There is no substitute for seeing it with your own eyes.</li>
    </ul>
